Secondary Sjogren's Syndrome (Mild Overlap)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Mild dry mucosa alongside transient arthralgia, managed conservatively

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-042
Reason for Visit: Acute severe dysphagia and sensation of a foreign body in the throat requiring exclusion of esophageal obstruction or acute severe allergic reaction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate a sudden inability to swallow and intense burning pain in the throat. Immediate clinical review was strictly necessary to rule out acute esophageal food bolus impaction, severe infectious pharyngitis, or anaphylactic angioedema prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My throat feels like it's glued shut. I tried to eat some dry crackers and now I can't swallow properly, and it burns intensely. I feel like I'm choking.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports sicca symptoms secondary to another mild autoimmune state (mild Rheumatoid Arthritis). Tonight, the patient experienced sudden, severe dysphagia while consuming dry food. The profound lack of saliva caused the food bolus to adhere to the pharyngeal mucosa, leading to an acute burning sensation and subjective feeling of airway obstruction. The patient is highly distressed and sipping water continuously without relief. Denies lip swelling, wheeze, or true difficulty breathing.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently unable to tolerate solid food.
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Alert, visibly anxious, frequently attempting to clear the throat and sipping water.
Physical Exam: Oropharyngeal exam reveals dry, erythematous mucosa without exudate. No pooling of saliva in the floor of the mouth. The airway is completely patent; no stridor or uvular edema. Mild dry mucosa alongside transient arthralgia, managed conservatively (noted in history). Patient demonstrates the ability to comfortably swallow a 30mL bolus of water during the exam without coughing or regurgitation. Neck exam is normal with no cervical lymphadenopathy.
Environmental Observations: Safe environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No recent commencement of new anticholinergic medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute symptomatic Xerostomia flare in Secondary Sjogren's Syndrome (Mild Overlap) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Esophageal Food Bolus Impaction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Demonstrated ability to comfortably swallow a liquid bolus without regurgitation, and pain localized to the oropharynx rather than retrosternal.

Acute Anaphylactic Angioedema - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of lip/tongue swelling, normal respiratory rate, and normal oxygen saturation.

Acute Streptococcal Pharyngitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, absence of tonsillar exudate, and no tender anterior cervical lymphadenopathy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe dysphagia and a subjective sensation of choking, highly suggestive of a mechanical food bolus impaction or acute angioedema. Triage correctly prioritized a physical examination to ensure a secure airway and exclude a structural blockage requiring emergency endoscopy. Clinical bedside assessment confirmed the airway was fully patent and the patient could tolerate clear fluids. The severe distress was entirely secondary to mechanical friction on profoundly dry mucosa (xerostomia). This timely evaluation safely averted an unnecessary emergency department transfer, provided immediate palliative reassurance, and entirely justified the need for urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered a trial of an over-the-counter artificial saliva substitute with good subjective relief. Instructed patient to strictly avoid dry, crumbly foods tonight.
Positive Behavioral Change: Use the artificial saliva spray directly on your tongue and inside your cheeks exactly 5 minutes before every meal. By actively lubricating the mouth prior to eating, food will slide down the esophagus smoothly without adhering to the dry tissue, immediately preventing the burning and gagging sensations.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 2-3 weeks to discuss long-term management of sicca symptoms and optimization of underlying autoimmune therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you become completely unable to swallow your own saliva, if you begin drooling uncontrollably, or if you develop a high-pitched whistling sound when you breathe in (stridor).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive regarding the nature of the flare; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The extreme dryness causes severe friction, but it does not cause your throat to physically close over. It requires aggressive, constant lubrication, particularly before meals.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek medical review if the inside of your mouth develops a thick white coating that does not wipe away, or if you develop severe swelling below your jaw.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your salivary glands are not producing enough fluid to break down dry foods, causing them to scrape your throat. There is no blockage and your airway is completely safe.
Actionable Steps: Avoid all dry, crumbly foods like crackers or toast. Chew sugar-free gum to stimulate any remaining saliva. Use artificial saliva sprays frequently throughout the day.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Severe dry mouth (Sjogren's flare). No food blockage or throat infection. Airway is completely safe. Use artificial saliva spray. See GP routinely.

SMS EDUCATION

Use artificial saliva spray before meals and avoid dry foods. Go to the ED if you cannot swallow water, start drooling, or get breathing difficulty.
